WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.220
Least privilege means granting only the access needed to perform an approved task.

2
00:00:05.320 --> 00:00:09.160
In PAM, that idea is practical rather than abstract.

3
00:00:09.260 --> 00:00:14.780
A database administrator may need production database administration, but not domain-wide administration.

4
00:00:14.880 --> 00:00:21.120
A help desk role may need endpoint support rights, but not the ability to change identity provider policy.

5
00:00:21.220 --> 00:00:26.820
A deployment system may need to update a service, but not read every secret in the environment.

6
00:00:27.100 --> 00:00:31.900
Standing privilege is one of the largest sources of unnecessary risk.

7
00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:43.360
If administrator rights are always available, a stolen session, compromised endpoint, malicious insider, or mistaken command can immediately affect sensitive systems.

8
00:00:43.460 --> 00:00:53.700
Just-in-time access reduces that exposure by granting elevation only when needed, for a defined purpose, for a limited time, and often with approval or additional authentication.

9
00:00:53.980 --> 00:00:56.700
Approval workflows should match risk.

10
00:00:56.800 --> 00:01:01.840
Low-risk elevation may be automatically granted when policy conditions are satisfied.

11
00:01:01.940 --> 00:01:11.140
High-risk access may require manager approval, system owner approval, change ticket validation, peer review, or security review.

12
00:01:11.240 --> 00:01:17.720
The workflow should capture purpose and evidence without turning urgent operational work into a maze.

13
00:01:17.820 --> 00:01:24.380
Good design reduces careless elevation while preserving a path for legitimate maintenance and response.

14
00:01:24.660 --> 00:01:27.460
Break-glass access is a special case.

15
00:01:27.560 --> 00:01:34.680
Organizations need emergency access when normal systems fail, but break-glass paths must be tightly controlled.

16
00:01:34.780 --> 00:01:41.180
They should be rare, strongly authenticated, monitored, documented, and reviewed after use.

17
00:01:41.280 --> 00:01:46.800
A break-glass account that is easy, shared, or invisible is not resilience.

18
00:01:46.900 --> 00:01:49.380
It is an unmanaged privilege path.

19
00:01:49.660 --> 00:01:56.220
Separation of duties helps prevent one person or process from controlling an entire sensitive workflow.

20
00:01:56.320 --> 00:02:00.640
The person requesting privileged access may not be the same person approving it.

21
00:02:00.740 --> 00:02:05.780
The identity administrator may not be the only reviewer of identity administration activity.

22
00:02:05.880 --> 00:02:10.040
Access reviews should test whether roles still match business need.

23
00:02:10.140 --> 00:02:18.300
Success looks like reduced standing privilege, clear elevation purpose, traceable approval, and timely removal when work is complete.
